Z||000001||Taijiguy||09-13-2001||05:36 PM||||I have to say, I'm glad to see all the patriotism being expressed recently. However, sadly, my opinion is that when it comes to that, most people in this country are hypocrites. Like people who only go to church on Easter. When it's the popular thing to do, everyone jumps on the bandwagon. In a few weeks, many will go back to their old habits and ways. Sitting and talking during the National Anthem at sporting events, not taking a moment to stop and thank a law enforcement officer or fire fighter. Seeing only the bad in our country. I'm cynical maybe. But I've been witness to this kind of behavior too many times. It'll be interesting to see how soon this sudden outbreak of national pride lasts. I consider myself very patriotic. Just about every man in my family served proudly in the Military, including uncles and cousins. I always want to stomp the ****  out of the inconsiderate morons who can't shut their pieholes for two minutes during the national anthem. (Me, my dad, and another guy almost got into a fistfight with a few other guys over that a few years back at the Dayton air show)<BR>I guess what I'm suggesting is, maybe it would be nice if this sense of pride could sustain for more than a few weeks.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fformula88||09-13-2001||08:08 PM||||I think I see it just the opposite... I think maybe Americans take their freedom for granted most of the time, when its business as usual, most people just go about their lives and stayed wrapped up in them.  However, when national tragedies such as this bring their patriotism and national pride out.<P>I suppose that maybe some of it is hypocracy... but I really think its more likely just taken for granted.  <P>Thanks for the lyrics!  Its a great song!<BR>||||reg||1
